Fall back to the service registry when no backup service has reported

Since the backup-host-selection series (acfc87e201 and its successors,
Stein) the scheduler picks the host of a new backup from
`HostManager.backup_service_states`
(cinder/scheduler/host_manager.py, `_get_available_backup_service_host`),
which only the capability report a backup service publishes over an RPC
fanout fills: once from `BackupManager.init_host`, then every
`backup_driver_stats_polling_interval` (60 s). Two cold-start orderings
leave the scheduler without a usable entry until the next report:

* The scheduler starts in the same second as the backup service. Its own
  `request_service_capabilities` goes out before the backup service's RPC
  server is bound, and the backup service's `init_host` report goes out
  before the scheduler's RPC server is bound. Both casts are dropped.
* The `init_host` report is sent while the backup driver still
  initializes in its own thread, so it carries `backend_state: False`,
  and `_list_backup_services` skips the entry.

Either way `SchedulerManager.create_backup` answers every backup in that
window with "Service not found for creating backup": the backup goes to
`error` and the volume back to `available`, although the service is up
and its heartbeat is in the registry. CobaltCore's e2e suite
cinder-backup-nfs hit the window on CI run 39046784189, 21 s after the
processes started, and a user who backs up a volume in the first minute
after a cold start of the control plane hits it the same way. The
scheduler offers no view of `backup_service_states`, so a client cannot
wait for the report either.

`_get_any_available_backup_service` now falls back to the service
registry when no report names a ready service and no driver was asked
for: `objects.ServiceList.get_all_by_topic` for the backup topic,
filtered by availability zone and `Service.is_up`, keyed by cluster name
or host like the reports. That is what `cinder.backup.api.API` reads for
restore and delete, and what the scheduler read before the reports
existed. A report, once received, keeps precedence, and a service whose
driver never initializes stops sending heartbeats and leaves the registry
after `service_down_time`. A driver constraint cannot be checked against
the registry, so that path still raises ServiceNotFound.

No upstream test pins the capability-only behaviour: the scheduler tests
mock `get_backup_host`, and test_host_manager.py never calls the backup
methods. The patch adds six tests to `HostManagerTestCase` in
cinder/tests/unit/scheduler/test_host_manager.py: the fallback, its
availability-zone and heartbeat filters, the cluster name, the not-ready
report, the driver case and the empty registry. Three of them raise
ServiceNotFound on the unpatched tree.

tests/container-images/verify_cinder.sh asserts both halves against the
built image with mocks: an empty report set resolves to the registered
service, and a report wins without consulting the registry.

Applies to cinder 29.0.0 (2026.2).

Upstream status: not yet proposed.

